# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored on purpose — see below.
#
# VITE_RETURN_ADDRESS — where employees' progress reports are emailed.
#
# NOT COMMITTED, AND NOT A SECRET EITHER. This repo is public, so a real address
# here would be scraped; that is the only reason it lives in `.env`. The address
# is printed on the employee's plan sheet, so it is not confidential — treat it
# as "do not publish", not "do not disclose".
#
# Set it per machine, and in the Vercel project's environment variables when the
# client build is deployed. Unset is a real, handled state: the app tells the
# employee reporting is not set up yet rather than opening a mail client with no
# recipient (sendReturn -> 'unconfigured').
#
# BUILD-TIME, DELIBERATELY. Plan §4.2 has the return address travelling in the
# plan QR. It does not, and there is an argument in HANDOFF 2026-08-31 that it
# should not: as a build setting, changing it costs one redeploy and phones pick
# it up on next load. Carried in the QR, changing it means re-issuing every
# outstanding plan. The recognition key is the part that genuinely needs the
# contract decision.
VITE_RETURN_ADDRESS=

# VITE_CLIENT_BASE_URL - where an employee's phone lands when it scans a plan QR.
#
# SET THIS, OR NO PLAN CAN BE ISSUED. There is no default any more. It is read
# only by the admin build's Issue page, and the admin build runs locally, on the
# machine doing the issuing -- it is NOT a Vercel setting, and changing it needs
# no redeploy. Restart `npm run dev` and the new value is live.
#
# It used to fall back to the admin's own origin, which was correct while one
# build served both halves. The client/admin split made that permanently wrong:
# the admin runs at http://localhost:5173, which is a perfectly valid base URL,
# so a QR built from it mints, prints and scans without complaint and fails for
# the first time in an employee's hands. Empty now refuses at the admin's screen
# instead. See src/lib/clientBaseUrl.js and test/clientBaseUrl.test.js.
#
# It is INSIDE the QR, so it spends capacity the plan needs: a long host can push
# an otherwise-fine plan over the limit. The issue page shows the byte count and
# the headroom for exactly that reason, and lets you edit the address per issue.
#
# The deployed client as of 2026-09-15:
VITE_CLIENT_BASE_URL=https://hma-cadence.vercel.app

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_CLIENT_NAME / VITE_CLIENT_LOGO — who this deployment is for.
#
# Added 2026-09-24. The employer was spelled into the markup in four places:
# the login card's site line and mark, and the mark in both app headers. Owner:
# "make both of those aspects configurable (to avoid potential hiccups in the
# future)."
#
# UNSET keeps today's values — "Hendrickson · Navarre" and
# /hendrickson-logo.jpg — so an existing build is unaffected. That is a
# deliberate difference from VITE_RETURN_ADDRESS above, whose absence has to be
# a handled state because a real address must not sit in a public repo. A
# client name and a logo are not secrets, and blanking the branding of the
# deployment that exists today to serve a future one that does not would be the
# wrong trade.
#
# EMPTY IS A REAL SETTING. Set either to "" and that piece disappears cleanly —
# no broken image, no stray divider — which is what a deployment for a client
# with no mark, or no site line, actually wants.
#
# The logo is a path into `public/`, so a new client drops their artwork there
# and points this at it. It wants black line art: it sits on a white plate,
# for the same reason ATI's POSITIVE lockup does (Brand Guide 1.3/1.5).
#
# VITE_CLIENT_NAME=Hendrickson · Navarre
# VITE_CLIENT_LOGO=/hendrickson-logo.jpg
